☐ Add `pending`, `queued`, and `waiting` as distinct deployment statuses (contract DeploymentStatus + OpenAPI + frontend tile/colors + fetcher StatusMapper) instead of collapsing to in-progress. All three are pre-`in_progress` GitHub states the fetcher currently flattens: `pending` = deployment created but not yet started/picked up (currently → in-progress); `queued` = queued to run (currently → in-progress); `waiting` = blocked on a deployment protection rule (required reviewers / wait timer) — currently DROPPED by the fetcher (no StatusMapper case). Surfacing them distinctly shows the real pre-run lifecycle — created-but-not-started, queued, and approval-gated/timer-blocked deployments (e.g. protected production environments) — instead of an undifferentiated "in-progress".
☐ Surface `cancelled` (and `not approved`/rejected) deployments distinctly instead of showing them as `failure`. ROOT CAUSE: GitHub's deployment_status `state` enum has NO `cancelled` value (closed set: error/failure/inactive/in_progress/queued/pending/success, +`waiting` in webhooks) — when an env-bound job is cancelled, GitHub writes a deployment_status of `failure`, so the fetcher's StatusMapper (`failure`→`failure`) faithfully shows "failed". The cancellation signal lives ONE LEVEL UP in the workflow-run/job `conclusion` (`cancelled`/`skipped`/`timed_out`), and "not approved" lives in the environment pending-deployment-reviews API (`state: rejected`) — neither is in the deployments-status pipeline today. NOT a mapper tweak. Work: (1) new contract status(es) `cancelled` [+ maybe `rejected`] in DeploymentStatus + OpenAPI + frontend tile/colors; (2) fetcher cross-references the associated workflow-run/job `conclusion` when a deployment hits `failure` and emits `cancelled` instead — the run is already cached per `(repo, run_id)` for service identity (F11), but the deployment→run→job correlation + conclusion read are new; (3) optionally read pending-deployment-reviews to detect `rejected` for `waiting` deployments. Also document the quirk in FETCHER_SPECIFICATION §5.3 mapping table (cancelled jobs arrive as `failure` by design) so it's not re-discovered as a "bug". Related to the `waiting`/`queued` item above — same shape: read BEYOND the deployment_status state.
☐ Fetcher SSO-403 diagnostic: detect GitHub `403` carrying an `X-GitHub-SSO` response header in GithubClient and emit an actionable, single-line log (e.g. "token not SAML-SSO-authorized for org <x> — Configure SSO → Authorize; re-authorize after rotation", with the header's authorization URL) instead of the generic EnsureSuccessStatusCode throw. A classic PAT that is unauthorized (or freshly rotated) returns 403, not 401; today it surfaces as an opaque error. Pairs with the classic-PAT/SSO adopter docs shipped in #256. Read-only diagnostic — no behavior change, no retry.
☐ Fetcher live poll re-ingests OLD status events beyond BACKFILL_DEPTH. After a correct depth-2 backfill, the normal poll later re-posts deployment-status events from 2–3h ago, overfilling the history drawer with old in-progress/success rows. Suspect: post-backfill per-repo cursor `since` high-water mark not bounding live re-reads, or the terminal-skip/ETag (§5.5.1/§5.5.2) reused-deployment map resurfacing old deployments whose statuses then re-post. Expected: live poll ingests only status events newer than the cursor's per-repo `since`. Found during rate-limit feature live testing (separate from that feature).
☐ Matrix view: let the user hide/show and reorder environment columns. Frontend-only — environments are currently server-sorted (Ordinal) and all rendered. Add a per-user column control (e.g. an environments picker mirroring the existing fields picker) to toggle column visibility and drag-reorder columns, with the preference persisted client-side (like the field-visibility toggles). No API/contract change; the matrix response still carries all environments — the view just filters/reorders them. [IN PROGRESS — PR #259]
☐ Refactor topbar to reuse components instead of hand-built duplicates (tech debt from the column-controls work, PR #259). Today `frontend/dashboard/src/app/shared/` has NO reusable UI components; topbar.component.html hand-builds the same "picker" 4× inline (rate-limit, correlation, columns, fields = `p-popover` → `.picker-content` → `.popover-title` shell) and the toggle-pill list 3× (Fields-matrix, Fields-swimlanes, Columns = `.field-grid`→`.field-toggle`→`.field-check`→`.field-label`), plus copy-pasted hidden-count badge + open/close logic per picker. Extract: (1) `ToggleListComponent` (checkbox-pill list — `items[]` + `(toggle)`, 1-or-2-col) reused by Fields×2 + Columns; (2) `PickerPopoverComponent` (icon-btn trigger + count badge + `p-popover` + title + content slot) reused by all 4 popovers; fold the badge into the trigger. Goal: the column controls become a composition of reused components with zero bespoke markup; topbar shrinks. The live-app Playwright guards added in #259 (drag, show/hide, inset, badges) are the regression net so the refactor can't silently break behavior/visuals. See memory: reuse-dont-reinvent.
